WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Time boundaries define when attention is available, when it is protected,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.280
and when it is off limits.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.320
Boundaries can apply to meetings, messages, focus blocks, work hours, recovery,

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.360
family commitments, and decision windows.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.180
A boundary is not a hostile wall.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.960
It is an agreement that helps people understand capacity and reliability.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:17.920
Without boundaries,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.695
availability can become a default that quietly consumes all other priorities.

00:00:21.695 --> 00:00:24.730
Calendar hygiene means keeping commitments visible and realistic.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.750
If focus time matters, it should appear somewhere.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.960
If a meeting needs preparation, that preparation should be counted.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.350
If a deadline requires review, the review should not be assumed to happen magically.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.840
A calendar is not just a record of meetings.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.115
It is an operating model for attention, commitments, and constraints.

00:00:39.115 --> 00:00:41.440
Meeting discipline protects the time of everyone involved.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.880
A useful meeting has a purpose, a scope, the right participants,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.640
and a decision or outcome that justifies the cost.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.875
A one-hour meeting with eight people is not one hour of time; it is eight working hours,

00:00:51.875 --> 00:00:53.640
plus preparation and recovery.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.160
Reducing unnecessary meetings is not antisocial.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.666
It is a basic form of professional respect.

00:00:59.666 --> 00:01:02.160
Commitments should be explicit, visible, and reviewable.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.360
Silent overcommitment often happens when people accept work without renegotiating scope,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.600
deadline, quality level, or existing responsibilities.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.220
Reliable people do not create reliability by pretending capacity is infinite.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.835
They create it by clarifying what is being promised, what tradeoffs are required,

00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:19.890
and when a commitment needs to be revised before it becomes a failure.
